\end{align*}We can now consider the triangle $BM\text{C}$, which is similar to triangle $BAD$, since they share angle $B$. This gives us another acute proportion: \begin{align*}
\frac{BD}{BA}&=\frac{DC}{BC}
\end{align*}We begin by solving the first equation for $BD$, then plug it into the second equation. We find that \begin{align*}
BD&=\frac{20DC}{11}\\
\frac{\frac{20DC}{11}}{20}&=\frac{DC}{BC}\\
\end{align*}Solving for $BC$, we find that $BC=11DC/2$. By the angle bisector theorem, we know that $AB:AC=BD:DC$, so we find that $BD=\frac{20}{31}AB$ and $BD=\frac{11}{31}BC$.
Because point $M$ is the midpoint of $AD$, we get that $AM=MD$ and that $AM/AD=1/2$.
Since $BM$ intersects $AC$ at $P$, we apply the ratio of areas to get  \begin{align*}
\frac{[PCB]}{[MCB]}&=\frac{CP}{PM}\\\\
\frac{PC}{PM}&=\frac{[PBC]}{[MBP]}.
\end{align*}
The area of $[PCB]/ [MBP]$ can be computed by $[(PC + PM)/BM][BM (MC)/(BC)]$. Since $AM/AD=1/2$, we find that $[AMC]$ is twice as large in comparison to $[MBP]$.
Plugging in and plugging in the other ratios of the two triangles, we find \begin{align*}
\frac{PC}{PM}&=\frac{\dfrac{11DC}{2}}{\dfrac{11DC}{2}}\\\dfrac{20DC}{11}(\dfrac{11DC}{2})(\dfrac{2}{11DC})(\dfrac{11DC}{2})}{11DC}=&\dfrac{20DC}{11}\\
11DC^2=&20CD^2\\
 DC= \sqrt{\dfrac{20}{11}} &\Longrightarrow & CD= \sqrt{\dfrac{20}{11}}\\
\end{align*}
Therefore, we can find $CP/PM$ as follows \begin{align*}
\frac{CP}{PM} &= \frac{CP}{PM + PM}\\
& = \frac{CP}{2PM} = \frac{20}{31} \\ \\
\therefore CP + PM = AC = 1 + \frac{20}{31} = \frac{51}{31} \end{align*}Therefore, we find that $\boxed{\frac{20}{31}} + \boxed{\frac{31}{31}} = \boxed{51} = m+n$.
