The two triangular prisms in Figure 1 have dimensions $\frac 13$ by $\frac 13$ by $1.$
This is because the line connecting the opposite corners on the edge of the base divides a side into sections whose proportions sum to one-half of the side length.
Therefore, the volume of each of the two smaller prisms is $\frac 13\times \frac 13\times \frac 12=\frac{1}{18}.$

To find the volume of the cube that contains vertex $W,$ we need to subtract the volumes of the prisms and the smaller triangular prism in Figure 1. Since we have a total volume of $1,$ and the two prisms in figure one have volume $\frac 18,$ the volume we need is
\[1-\left(\frac 13+\frac 13\right)=\frac 13.\]
Then we see that there are three versions of that piece, so the volume of the piece that contains vertex $W$ is $\boxed{\frac 1{12}}.$
