solution:
Recall that $(-a)^n=-a^n$ when $n$ is odd so we can rewrite $(-5)^5$ as $-5^5$. Because $a^m\div a^n=a^{m-n}$ for positive integers $m>n$, we get $$-5^5\div5^3=-5^{5-3} =-5^2.$$ Now we can rewrite the expression to get  \begin{align*}
(-5)^5\div5^3+3^{4}-6^{1}&=-5^2+3^4-6\\
&=-25+81-6\\
&=\boxed{50}.
\end{align*}

response:
We have $(-5)^5=-5^5=(-5\cdot -5\cdot -5\cdot -5\cdot -5)=-3125$, $5^3=5\cdot 5\cdot 5=125$, $3^4=3\cdot 3\cdot 3\cdot 3=81$, and $6^1=6$.  Putting it all together, we get \begin{align*}
(-5)^5\div5^3+3^{4}-6^{1}&=(-3125)\div 125+81-6\\
&=-25+81-6\\
&=\boxed{50}.
\end{align*}
